# Go Standards

Write Go to pass `codeality-go gate` the first time: format, `go mod tidy`,
golangci-lint under the strict `.golangci.yml` that `codeality-go init` writes,
the structural rules, duplicates, `go test -race -shuffle=on` with a coverage
floor, and `govulncheck`. A new module starts with
`codeality-go init --apply --ci`. Read `.golangci.yml` for the exact linters and
limits; it is the authority, this file is the shape to aim for.

## The file unit

This replaces the general one-declaration-per-file rule for `.go` files, by the
owner's decision of 2026-11-23: Go attaches behaviour to types and keeps small
helpers beside their only caller.

- A file holds one exported unit: a type with its constructor, methods, typed
  constants and interface assertion; or one function; or one exported constant
  or variable.
- The file is the snake case of the unit: `Lease` in `lease.go`, `HTTPServer` in
  `http_server.go`, `NewPlan` and `Plan` both in `plan.go`.
- Unexported helpers stay in the file of their only caller. A helper a second
  file needs becomes a unit with a file of its own.
- `lease_test.go` sits beside `lease.go`. Helpers shared by the tests of a
  package live in the test file of the unit they build.
- At most 200 lines of code per file, 400 per test file.
- No `util`, `helpers`, `common`, `misc`, `shared` or `types` as a package or a
  file: name the thing by what it provides.
- An exception is an `overrides` entry in `codeality-go.yaml` with its reason,
  never `//nolint`.

## Packages

- Everything sits under `internal/` except `cmd/<binary>/main.go` and packages
  declared public in `codeality-go.yaml`.
- A package is named after its directory, a short lower-case noun, and has a
  `doc.go` with its package comment.
- `main` only wires: parse flags, build dependencies, call a package, exit.
- Accept interfaces, return concrete types. Declare an interface in the package
  that consumes it, as small as that consumer needs.
- No package-level mutable state and no `init`: pass dependencies through
  constructors.
- SQL lives in `.sql` files loaded with `embed`, never in string literals.

## Errors

- Return errors; panic only for a programmer error that cannot be handled.
- Wrap an error that crosses a package boundary with what was being done:
  `fmt.Errorf("lease job %s: %w", id, err)`.
- Sentinel errors are `var ErrNotFound = errors.New(...)`; error types end in
  `Error`. Compare with `errors.Is` and `errors.As`, never `==` or a string.
- Handle an error once: return it or log it, not both.

## Context and concurrency

- `ctx context.Context` is the first parameter of anything that blocks or does
  I/O. Never store it in a struct.
- Every HTTP request, query and subprocess carries a context with a deadline.
- Whoever starts a goroutine knows how it stops: a context, a closed channel or
  a `sync.WaitGroup`. `errgroup` for a group that fails together.
- The sender closes a channel. Shared state is guarded by a mutex declared just
  above the fields it protects.
- Pointer receivers for a type that is mutated or holds a lock, and then on
  every method of that type.

## Tests

- Package `x_test`, through the exported API; an in-package test only for what
  the API cannot reach.
- Table-driven with `t.Run`, `t.Parallel()` in the test and each subtest,
  `t.Helper()` in helpers, `t.TempDir()` and `t.Context()` for resources.
- Fakes written by hand against the consumer's interface; no mocking framework.
- Standard library `testing` and `cmp`-style comparison; failure messages read
  `Got(%v) = %v, want %v`.

## Style

- Every exported name has a doc comment that starts with the name and ends with
  a period.
- Guard clauses and early returns; the happy path stays at the left margin.
- `log/slog` with constant messages and key-value attributes.
- Standard library first: `slices`, `maps`, `cmp`, `errors.Join`, range over
  integers, `net/http` routing patterns. Add a dependency for what it cannot do.
